on  engineers could do experiments and  analyze fMRI data to make discoveries  about the brain and we're at a similar  point in intracranial EEG right now most  labs  using a hodgepodge of software tools  that they've downloaded from the  internet or MATLAB code that they've  written themselves and this presents a  serious obstacle to progress because  grad students or postdocs or PI's have  to spend time writing MATLAB code which  maybe they're not well equipped to do or  develop statistical tasks instead of  analyzing data and making discoveries  about the brain so our goal is to create  a purpose-built software package that's  designed solely for intracranial EEG and  has the flexibility necessary to make  discoveries about the brain and our  development goals for rave our most  important goal is to keep users close to  the data because the brain is so  complicated and there's so much we don't  know about it it's absolutely critical  that you have the ability to examine raw  data and make sure that you're not  misled by artifacts there can be  artifacts in intracranial EEG data like  in any other kind of data and if you are  only looking at the final product of the  analysis which a long analysis pipeline  might spit out you might not realize  that some are along the analysis  pipeline one of the steps had gone awry  and the final map that you're looking at  is absolutely meaningless so the good  thing is that computers are so rapid now  that we can do real-time interactive and  data analysis without leaving it to run  overnight which you might have had to do  20 years ago with fMRI data so you can  easily update your analysis in real-time  and see what happens and this makes it  easy to analyze interest if you have the  right software tool it makes it much  more efficient to analyzed data second  we want to have rigorous statistical  methodology again by analogy with fMRI  there's been periodic paroxysms of  controversy and fMRI for instance in the  early days subjects were treated as  fixed effects when it soon be eventually  became clear that really no subjects  were not all identical they needed to be  treated as random effects and many other  kinds of statistical arguments so the  part of this comes because people are  making arbitrary choices who have not  necessarily had any training statistics  and are just using a tool box or a  MATLAB function so by building a  purpose-built program we can make wise  choices and encourage people to do it  now  see the right way of course we're not  prescriptive but we can say okay the  built-in way that rave analyzes data has  been vetted by statisticians and decided  that it's a rigorous way to analyze data  and the final goal of rave development  is to make sure that it plays well with  others that is to say that it allows you  to work with existing tools that you  might have in MATLAB or C or Python and  integrate it into your existing  processing pipeline so that you don't  have to give up all the tools that you  already know and love so the current  state of rave development is that we  have more than 10 statistical modules  over 30,000 lines of code in the  codebase and it's being used on a daily  basis by many labs at Baylor College of  Medicine and of course we would love for  other users to use it as well and you  can download it for free from github or  from the website that's shown on this  slide and we're working on rave every  day to try to improve it so in the  second part of the talk you're going to  hear my colleague going through some of  the awesome features of rave and we're  going to demonstrate it on some real  intracranial EEG data that we collected  and that was published in a paper in  Elife in 2018 and I'll just briefly  introduce that dataset to you to make it  easier to understand when you see it  analyzed within rave so this is a study  where we presented patients with three  kinds of stimuli these were short video  clips of words so as you can see in t