ich we're going to  do our statistical comparisons so the  final plot on the bottom right this  shows you for each individual trial this  is the average power within that red  window so each point here is the trial  response to a particular stimulus and by  default for a single condition we can  just compare that activity to zero to  see if this electrode had a significant  response to these auditory only stimuli  now of course we can go back up to the  top and change the electrode we're  looking at for example what if we look  at an electrode that's in the auditory  cortex or see me as a visual cortex so  here electrode 78 that's in the visual  cortex we can see that there's not  really a strong response at all to the  auditory signal you know very slightly  elevated above zero but compared to the  previous channel there's really not  anything going on however what if we  look at the visual responses to the  visual stimuli so I can go over to the  trial selector and start to add in these  other trial types  click all of these I can apply a label  call these visual  and what you can immediately see in the  is that when we add in a second group of  trials and that rave now shows these  side by side and so although there isn't  a very strong response in to the  auditory stimuli there's a very strong  response to the visual only stimuli so  these are the stimuli where you just see  the person talking but you don't hear  anything and when we go back down and  look at the time series now we see both  time series overlay and then in the  bottom right graph we can see that here  there's a much stronger response to the  visual trials compared to the auditory  only trials and so we could do this for  all the electrodes that we've loaded but  rave also has ability to take this  contrast that we've defined this  auditory versus visual contrast and  apply it to every electrode that has  been loaded on the brain so this takes a  few seconds but I've already pre-loaded  this calculation and so here you can see  really nice the topographic organization  of this contrast so towards the bottom  right in the superior temporal gyrus  there's a lot of red so these are  regions of the brain that respond more  to the auditory stimulus than to the  visual only stimulus as we rotate the  brain and look at the back of the brain  we can see that there's a lot more blue  and so these electrodes are responding  more to the visual stimulus than to the  auditory stimulus now using rave I can  click on these different blue channels  so this blue one here in the middle is  electrode 78 and that allows us to then  scroll up and see okay yes that's the  electrode that we're looking at now  electrode 78 but we can find other  electrodes that we want to inspect so  electrode 22 is the electrode we were  looking at initially but let's look over  at for instance electrode 23  so we can simply type in 23 into the  electrode screen and rave automatically  updates to show you the new calculations  for this new electrode and what's  interesting if we scroll down and look  at the time series we can see that  although the response the auditory only  is much higher than the response  dividual only there does seem to be a  response to visual only trials and so  what you might want to do is what if we  delete the trials for auditory only now  rave will update to only show the visual  only trials and if we look at the  comparison here although the response is  smaller it's still significant so in the  bottom right panel we can see again the  individual trial plots and the mean of  this is 63% so there is a significant  change significant response to visual  stimuli  even though auditory is much stronger  and this accords with what we know about  this region of the posterior superior  temporal gyrus as being multi-sensory  and so it's responding both to auditory  alone and visual alone stimuli and so  you could repeat this process with all  of your different subjects we don'