ich we're going to  do our statistical comparisons so the  final plot on the bottom right this  shows you for each individual trial this  is the average power within that red  window so each point here is the trial  response to a particular stimulus and by  default for a single condition we can  just compare that activity to zero to  see if this electrode had a significant  response to these auditory only stimuli  now of course we can go back up to the  top and change the electrode we're  looking at for example what if we look  at an electrode that's in the auditory  cortex or see me as a visual cortex so  here electrode 78 that's in the visual  cortex we can see that there's not  really a strong response at all to the  auditory signal you know very slightly  elevated above zero but compared to the  previous channel there's really not  anything going on however what if we  look at the visual responses to the  visual stimuli so I can go over to the  trial selector and start to add in these  other trial types  click all of these I can apply a label  call these visual  and what you can immediately see in the  is that when we add in a second group of  trials and that rave now shows these  side by side and so although there isn't  a very strong response in to the  auditory stimuli there's a very strong  response to the visual only stimuli so  these are the stimuli where you just see  the person talking but you don't hear  anything and when we go back down and  look at the time series now we see both  time series overlay and then in the  bottom right graph we can see that here  there's a much stronger response to the  visual trials compared to the auditory  only trials and so we could do this for  all the electrodes that we've loaded but  rave also has ability to take this  contrast that we've defined this  auditory versus visual contrast and  apply it to every electrode that has  been loaded on the brain so this takes a  few seconds but I've already pre-loaded  this calculation and so here you can see  really nice the topographic organization  of this contrast so towards the bottom  right in the superior temporal gyrus  there's a lot of red so these are  regions of the brain that respond more  to the auditory stimulus than to the  visual only stimulus as we rotate the  brain and look at the back of the brain  we can see that there's a lot more blue  and so these electrodes are responding  more to the visual stimulus than to the  auditory stimulus now using rave I can  click on these different blue channels  so this blue one here in the middle is  electrode 78 and that allows us to then  scroll up and see okay yes that's the  electrode that we're looking at now  electrode 78 but we can find other  electrodes that we want to inspect so  electrode 22 is the electrode we were  looking at initially but let's look over  at for instance electrode 23  so we can simply type in 23 into the  electrode screen and rave automatically  updates to show you the new calculations  for this new electrode and what's  interesting if we scroll down and look  at the time series we can see that  although the response the auditory only  is much higher than the response  dividual only there does seem to be a  response to visual only trials and so  what you might want to do is what if we  delete the trials for auditory only now  rave will update to only show the visual  only trials and if we look at the  comparison here although the response is  smaller it's still significant so in the  bottom right panel we can see again the  individual trial plots and the mean of  this is 63% so there is a significant  change significant response to visual  stimuli  even though auditory is much stronger  and this accords with what we know about  this region of the posterior superior  temporal gyrus as being multi-sensory  and so it's responding both to auditory  alone and visual alone stimuli and so  you could repeat this process with all  of your different subjects we don't have  time to show you today how rave is  organized but there is a select Data  button that allows you to quickly choose  which subjects that you want to load and  this is organized by projects and so  I'll just show you in the drop down here  based on which project you want to load  we're looking out the congruence data  set but you can see we have lots and  lots of data sets that were you we've  analyzed with rave and even within this  congruence data set we've already  analyzed lots of different subjects so  we are using Ray's on a day to day basis  the last step that you might want to do  in your analysis is to try to look at  some of these comparisons across all of  your subjects so I'll jump over here to  the group analysis module on the far  left and choose the linear mix effects  model so we've loaded up in our mixed  sex model module all of our subjects  from the congruence data set and what  rave allows you to do here in this model  building panel in the middle left is  actually choose how you want to model  your data and so we'll model our data  with the dependent variable being power  look at a fixed effects of condition so  it's going to compare auditory versus  audio-visual it's also going to include  some other trial types I've had in this  experiment here it's showing you in the  formula panel the exact formula that's  being used and so this way if you need  to report in a paper  how are my statistics actually done you  can just you can say this is exactly the  command that was run we choose run  analysis and depending on how much data  you have it may take you know faster or  slower and we can see here that the LME  output panel rave now reports everything  you need to know to compare condition  across all of your subjects and it  appropriately takes into account both  the fact that subjects are random effect  but also that electrodes are nested  within subject and so this is part of  our goal to present more modern  statistical techniques as just the way  things are just the way rave works so  we've shown you today just to reiterate  that in rave we have a modular interface  that allows you to compare conditions  using power voltage phase and then once  you've made those analyses you can  export them to the group level and then  see if your the differences that you see  at the individual electrodes are  consistent across patients thank you  you  thank you gentlemen for that  presentation of this cutting edge worm  as a reminder our speaker will follow up  with any questions about their  presentation by email  coming up next dr. BJ Hasan  he will present human brain science the  intersection of transitional nerve  technology and systems neuroscience with  a heart to seeing you there
 

